  notification design actually needs — inventing a plausible task system in my head and specifying against it is where
  this goes wrong.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What is this repo relative to the app you want notifications in? → Greenfield — design both[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Greenfield it is. I'll scope the task model to the minimum the notification design needs and no further.

  [1mNext fork: who "users" are.[0m "Tasks they care about" only means something if there's more than one person — otherwise
  every task is yours and there's nothing to subscribe to. This decides whether we need a server, auth, and a database
  at all, and it's the expensive one to change later.

  - [3mSingle-user, browser-only[0m — tasks in localStorage, notifications are in-app reminders (due dates, your own changes
    across tabs). No backend, no auth, ships fast. Rules out ever notifying someone [3melse[0m without a rewrite.
  - [3mMulti-user, one shared server[0m — real accounts, tasks with assignees/watchers, changes by one person notify another.
    This is what your sentence describes. Costs a backend, a datastore, and auth before any notification code exists.
  - [3mMulti-user, but stubbed auth for now[0m — real data model with [38;5;153muser_id[39m everywhere and a fake login. Keeps the shape
    correct so real auth drops in later, without building it today. Cheap to upgrade, unlike option one.

  I'd recommend the third. Your phrasing — "users," "tasks they care about," "change" implying someone else changed it —
  only makes sense multi-user, but building real auth first would mean a lot of work before you see a single
  notification. Stubbing the login while keeping [38;5;153muser_id[39m honest in the data model gets you the real design without the
  detour.
